NEEDS YOU
================================================================================

1. `wk <cmd> -h` is the leading comment block of `cmd/<name>` (`explain_cmd`,
   the dispatcher), so it counts against the 15% ceiling and cannot come down
   without changing what a user reads. Command bodies are at ~17%; the headers
   add about 8 points -- 632 lines in cmd/n-z alone. The argumentative prose in
   them is cut; the option tables, exit codes and env-var documentation are
   kept. Thin those too, and if so which (`wk bench -h` is 129 lines, ~30 of
   them design essay)? [decision]

   Thin everything; help should be concise and to-the point, using standard techinical documentation style. -h blocks can have more comments, but in general, code portions themselves should be under 5% comments. Variable names and overall structure should explain what is happening, not prose.

2. `claude/skills/fix-webkit-ews` and `claude/skills/jsc-review` tell an agent
   to run `gh pr view` and `gh api`. Reading now works through the injector, so
   those instructions run. Writing is uneven rather than refused: where `gh`
   posts to an endpoint `git-webkit` also uses -- `gh pr create`, `gh pr
   comment`, `gh pr review` -- the table has it and push-on lets it through,
   while `gh pr edit` (a PATCH on a pull request, which webkitscmpy does as a
   POST) and everything else is 403. Say reads only in those skills, add the
   verbs `gh` uses to the table, or leave the unevenness? [decision]

   It should be always read-only, and all writes should be accepted when push is enabled. Push on means a human is watching everything, so there is no need for any restrictions. Otherwise, it should be read-only for agents.

